Preserve the exact original `encoded(canonical(value))` bytes and error behavior: sort_keys=True, indent=2, default ASCII escaping, original integer/float tokens including signed zero, separators and empty-container layout, UTF-8 encoding, and exactly one final newline. Original canonical conversion remains responsible for NumPy conversion, subclass hooks, raw cyclic/deep traversal and its failures.

The fast domain has fixed inclusive limits:

- Exact built-in dict/list containers; exact str keys; exact str, bool, None, int and float values only. Integers have bit_length at most256; floats must be finite. Subclasses and unsupported canonical values are ineligible without invoking conversion hooks.
- Root containers have depth1; maximum container depth32. Scalars/keys do not add container depth. Maximum131,072 nodes, counting the root, every value occurrence and every dictionary-key occurrence. Maximum4,194,304 Unicode characters across all string-key and string-value occurrences. Aliased acyclic children are fully revisited and counted for each occurrence.
- Detect a repeated container on the active ancestor path as a cycle; ineligible values take original encoded(snapshot), without truncation, coercion, a new rejection exception or a changed process recursion/integer-conversion limit.

For eligible snapshots only, the pinned stdlib C compact encoder generates tokens with sort_keys=True, ensure_ascii=True, allow_nan=False and separators(',', ':'). A single whitespace formatter recognizes complete JSON string tokens, preserves all scalar/string tokens verbatim and formats punctuation outside strings to the original indent2 layout. No naive punctuation replacement inside strings, custom numeric formatter, third-party JSON package or alternative candidate. Unexpected compact-encoder/formatter exceptions propagate; no catch-and-fallback retry. The fallback decision precedes these calls.

